## 2.2. Synthesis of NiCo precursor@CNT composites
CNT powder was firstly treated with a mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ with a volume ratio of 3:2 at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ . The functionalized CNT was washed with distilled water and ethanol several times and then dried at $60\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.20\ \mathrm{mg}$ of as-treated CNT was ultrasonically dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol for standby. Next, $36.35~\mathrm{mg~Ni(NO_{3})_{2}}\cdot$ $.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $72.76\ \mathrm{mg\Co(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ dissolved in the mixed solution of $8~\mathrm{mL}$ glycerol and $30~\mathrm{mL}$ isopropanol. After adding $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of CNT dispersion into the above-mixed solution and stirring for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the mixture was placed into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon lined autoclave and reacted for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $180^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The black precipitate of NiCo precursor@CNT composites was washed with ethanol.
## 2.3. Synthesis of NiCo2O4/CoP@P-CNT film
## 2.4. Material characterizations
X-ray diffraction (XRD) with $\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha=1.54056\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (D/Max-RA Xray diffractometer) was used to characterize the crystallinity and phase purity. The surface and section morphologies of the electrode were identified by a field emission SEM (FESEM, Japan Hitachi S4800). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEM2010HR) were employed to examine the intrinsic features of the morphologies. Further oxidation states of the elements and surface chemical composition of the synthesized materials were examined by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) equipped an X-ray source of 150 WAI (ESCALAB 250X America). The EPR signal was tested by a Bruker A300-10/12 EPR spectrometer at room temperatures. The content of CNT in the sample is obtained by thermo gravimetry-differential scanning calorimetry (TGA/DSC, STA 449C Jupiter).
## 2.5. Electrochemical measurement
The performance of all the electrodes was evaluated by an electrochemical station (CHI 760E) at room temperature. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves, galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) curves, and the electrochemical impedance spectrometry (EIS) analyses were carried out to study the electrochemical performance using a beaker-type three-electrode system, where the prepared film employed as a working electrode, platinum wire as a counter electrode, and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ as a reference electrode. 6 M KOH aqueous solution is used as the electrolyte. In a two-electrode system, $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT and PGC were employed as the positive and negative electrodes, respectively.
## 3. Result and discussion
The synthesis of P-doped carbon nanotubes intercalated with $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}/C o P}$ core–shell polyhedron film is described in Fig. 1. Carbon nanotubes with high electrical conductivity and interconnected architecture were chosen as a substrate. As shown in Fig. 1, NiCo precursor nanospheres@carbon nanotubes composites were prepared using solvothermal method. Secondly, NiCo precur$s_{0\Gamma}@{\cal C}\mathrm{NT}$ was immersed into 2-methylimidazole and filtered a NiCo precursor/ZIF-67@CNT membrane. Thirdly, $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P-}$ -
CNT composite film was obtained by a two-step calcination process.
The surface and cross-section morphology of the synthesized composite film was examined by using an SEM analysis. The NiCo precursor/CNT SEM images in Fig. 2a show that the NiCo precursor with a sphere structure and CNT are evenly wound together. This tight binding provides a good precursor for in-situ growth of MOFs. Fig. 2b-d are the SEM images of $\Nu\mathrm{i}C o_{2}0_{4}/C o_{3}0_{4}@C\Nu\mathrm{T}-1$ , $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{CNT}-3$ , and $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}}@\mathsf{C N T-6}$ , respectively. With the increase of reaction time from 1 h to $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ , the surface particles gradually increase, and the crystals with better crystallinity are formed. Fig. 2d indicated that the carbon nanotubes are inserted into the metal oxide through the fracture, resulting in the self-supporting structure and improved conductivity. After further phosphating the sample $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}}@\mathsf{C N T}-3$ , the morphology of the metal compound remains unchanged, and the film still maintains good integrity (Fig. 2e). Fig. 2e is the section SEM image of $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P@P}}.$ -CNT with a thickness of about $20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}$ polyhedrons can be clearly observed on the surface of the film. Fig. 2f demonstrates the good integrity of the composite film and could be employed as a self-supporting electrode.
The TEM analysis was performed for the $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}/C o P@P-C I}$ NTs composite material. The polyhedral core–shell structure can be observed from Fig. 3a, where the outer structure presents a fluffy polyhedron structure, and the interior is a solid sphere. This is due to the formation of ZIF-67 on the surface of NiCo precursor when reacting with 2-MIM. After further calcination, ZIF-67 transformed into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ fluffy polyhedron, and NiCo precursor transformed into a solid $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core. The high-magnification TEM image in Fig. 3b shows that the CNT extends from the polyhedron, indicating the close combination of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}$ and carbon tubes, which not only provides a substrate for self-supporting electrode but also helps to improve the conductivity of the composites. Besides, due to the ultrasound treatment during the TEM sample preparation process, it is also observed that the scattered CoP and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanospheres were closely combining with the carbon nanotubes (Fig. S1). The HRTEM image in Fig. 3c displays the clear lattice fringes with an inter fringe spacing of $0.25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $0.22{\mathrm{~nm}}$ , which correspond to the (311) plane of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and (211) plane of CoP. Fig. $3\mathrm{d-g}$ are the elemental mapping images of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/$ CoP@P-CNT composites, proving the composites of Co, Ni, O, and P elements. P is distributed on polyhedron and carbon nanotubes,
Co is distributed on the whole polyhedron, while Ni is mainly distributed in the central nuclear structure, which further proves that the material we synthesized is $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT composites.
The crystallographic structure of the composite film was characterized by XRD (Fig. 4a), where the diffraction peaks at $31.6^{\circ}$ , $36.4^{\circ}$ , $48.3^{\circ}$ and $52.3^{\circ}$ marked by blue were well assigned to (011), (102), (211) and (103) planes of CoP (JCPDS 89–4862), indicating the successful synthesis of CoP polyhedrons. The orange marked several peaks at $18.9^{\circ}$ , 31.2, $36.7^{\circ}$ , $38.4^{\circ}$ , $44.6^{\circ}$ , $55.4^{\circ}$ , $59.1^{\circ}$ and $64.8^{\circ}$ were attributed to the (1 1 1), (2 2 0), (3 3 1), (2 2 2), (4 0 0), (422), (511), and (440) planes of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS 73-1702), respectively. XPS was furtherly employed to explore the chemical states of the synthesized $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\mathscr{@}\mathsf{P}.$ -CNT sample. The entire XPS survey spectrum in Fig. 4b verified the presence of Ni 2p, Co 2p, O 1s, C 1s, and P 2p. Ni 2p XPS spectrum can be fitted into two vibration peaks of two $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ and two vibration orbits (represented as ‘‘sat”) in Fig. 4c. Two strong peaks at $873.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $856.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ are the typical characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ . Another two peaks at $875.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $857.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ [26,27]. Similar to Ni, as represented in Fig. 4d, Co $\mathsf{2p}$ spectrum can also be divided into two kinds of vibration peaks of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ . Two peaks at $798.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $782.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $C o^{2+}$ , while another two peaks at 797.3 eV and $781.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ [28,29]. The O1s spectrum in Fig. 4e is fitted into three peaks centered at $533.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , 533.0 eV, and $531.6\mathrm{eV}$ , which were indexed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}0$ from carbon nanotubes, oxygen vacancies or defects, and surface-adsorbed water molecules, respectively [30,31]. Besides, the peak at $529.8\ \mathrm{\eV}$ can be attributed to the PAO bonds from the by-product [32]. The $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ spectrum in Fig. 4f is deconvoluted into three peaks of $\mathsf{P}{-}0$ $,133.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ , P $2\mathsf{p}_{2/1}$ $134.5\mathrm{eV})$ , and $\mathsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{2/3}$ $(133.3\mathrm{eV})$ [33,34]. The C1s spectrum. contains four fitted peaks at 284.25 eV, 284.80 eV, 285.37 eV, and $287.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ , assigning to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , CAO, CAP, and $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ bonds (Fig. 4g) [35–38]. The presence of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-P}}$ and $_{0-\mathrm{P}}$ peaks indicates the successful doping of P atom into the composite. The specific surface area of the as-prepared $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT was investigated by nitrogen adsorption–desorption measurements, as shown in Fig. 4h. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) results show that the material has a high specific surface area of $82.6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , in addition, the average pore diameter is found nearly $15.3\mathrm{nm}$ by the pore-size distribution analysis (Fig. 4h). Such a large specific surface area can provide more active sites for electrochemical reaction, appropriate pore size distribution is also conducive to the transmission of electrolyte. EPR is always used to examine unpaired electrons in materials, it is used here to investigate the oxygen vacancies in $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P@P}}$ -CNT electrode (Fig. 4i). Obviously, there is an symmetrical EPR signal at $\mathbf{g}=2.002$ for $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P-}$ CNT, but $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf C o_{3}.$ - $\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C N T}-3$ does not, indicating the intensive paramagnetic centers in oxygen-deficient $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT [18,20]. This result shows that the presence of oxygen vacancies after the phosphating reaction, which is beneficial to improve the electrochemical activities of the electrode material such as enhanced conductivity and improved the diffusion of carriers, as well as the adsorption capacity of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ .
Three-electrode set-up was employed to evaluate the electrochemical properties of the prepared electrodes using $6.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH aqueous electrolyte. The electrochemical performance of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{CNT}-1$ , $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}}\ @\mathsf{C N T-3}$ , $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}@C N T-6}$ , and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P}.$ -CNT were investigated and compared. Fig. 5a recorded the CV curves of $\Nu\mathrm{i}C o_{2}0_{4}/C o_{3}0_{4}@C\Nu\mathrm{T}-1$ , $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf C o_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{CNT}-3$ , $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}@C N T-6}$ , and $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT electrodes at a constant scan rate of $2{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ in the potential range of $0\ V{-}0.5\ V$ . Distinct redox peaks were observed in the positive and negative sweeps due to the reversible transformations of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ redox couple. $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CoP}@\mathrm{P}-($ CNT electrode demonstrates the largest CV area and maximum specific capacitance at the same scanning rate. Fig. 5b is the comparison of GCD curves of the four samples at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P-}$ CNT exhibits the longest charge–discharge time compared to $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}@C N}$ T-based electrodes, indicating its highest capacitance. Besides, other electrochemical characteristics were further measured for the $\Nu\mathrm{i}{\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P-C N}\rceil$ electrode. The CV curves of $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\mathscr{@}\mathsf{P}.$ -CNT electrode were recorded at different sweep rates from 2 to $100\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ in Fig. 5c, where the redox peaks represent a battery-like charge-storage process of the electrode. The corresponding electrochemical reactions are shown below: [25,36,39]
$$
\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}\ +\ O H^{-}\ +H_{2}O\ \leftrightarrow\ N i O O H\ +\ 2C o O O H\ +\ e^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CoOOH}+\mathrm{OH^{-}\leftrightarrow C o O_{2}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{e^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CoP}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{CoPOH}+\mathrm{e^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CoPOH}+\mathrm{OH^{-}\leftrightarrow C o P O}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{e^{-}}
$$
The GCD curves of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P}.$ -CNT at various current densities are presented in Fig. 5d, where the apparent voltage plateaus indicate its battery-type behavior. Besides, even at higher scan rates and current densities, the redox peaks in a CV and nonlinear GCD shapes still retained, suggesting the good rate capability of the $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P-}$ CNT electrode. The electrochemical characters of all other electrodes are presented in Fig. S2. The gravimetric capacities were calculated for all the above electrodes at different current densities in Fig. 5e, where the $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}/C o P@P-C N}$ T electrode demonstrated the highest values. Besides, Fig. 5f presents the capacity of $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\mathscr{@}\mathsf{P}.$ CNT electrode at different current densities. The highest gravimetric, volumetric, and area capacitance of $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P-}$ CNT can reach up to $1918.4~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , $1074.3\ensuremath{\mathrm{F}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ , and $2.1\:\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. EIS tests were also performed on the $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}}\ @\mathsf{C N T-3}$ and $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P@P-C N T}$ electrodes with insets showing the equivalent circuit diagram, as exhibited in Fig. S3 [40,41]. The numeric value of EIS is shown in Table S1. All the curves show an unobtrusive semicircle in the high-frequency range and a line at the low-frequency range. The semicircle diameter shows the resistance for charge transfer, and the line’s slope is related to the resistance of ion diffusion. It is found that the charge transfer resistance of $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}/C o P@P-C N}$ T $\left(3.99~\Omega\right)$ is smaller than that of $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o_{3}O_{4}}\ @\mathsf{C N T-3}$ $\left(4.96~\Omega\right)$ , indicating a better conductivity of samples after phosphating. Moreover, $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P-}$ CNT electrode shows a line almost vertical to the real axis, indicating its structure is beneficial to accelerate the electrolyte flow dynamics within the electrode [42] (Table 1).
The schematic diagram of energy storage mechanism of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}$ - $0_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P}$ -CNT is shown in Fig. 6a. The high gravimetric, volumetric, and area capacitance of the proposed self-support $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/$ ${\mathsf{C o P@P}}.$ -CNT film electrode mainly comes from the following four aspects. Firstly, the P-doped carbon nanotubes are evenly interpenetrated in $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}$ and formed a porous network structure, which can improve the electron/ion transfer rate in the whole composite material. Secondly, more active sites are supplied for redox reactions owing to the synergy between $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and CoP can supply and improve the wettability of the electrode. Thirdly, CoP polyhedron has a fluffy structure, which is conducive to the rapid diffusion of electrolyte and increase electrolyte/electrode interfacial contact, further accelerating the ions migration and electron transportation. Fourthly, $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P}.$ CNT has rich oxygen vacancies, which is beneficial to improve the electrochemical activities of the electrode material such as the enhanced conductivity and improved the diffusion of carriers, to further promote the oxidation–reduction reaction. The electrochemical behavior of the $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\varpi\mathsf{P}$ -CNT electrode was further explored and analyzed by the CV curves at a low potential window and low scan rates are presented in Fig. 6b. Through analyzing the scan rate dependence of the peak currents and peak potentials, the electrochemical process can be distinguished, diffusion or pseudocapacitance behavior. The peak current $I_{\mathbf{p}}$ (A) and scan rate $\nu\:(\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}),$ 1 obey the relationship as follows [43]:
$$
\log I_{\mathrm{p}}=\mathrm{~b~}\log\nu+\mathrm{~l0g~a~}
$$
The b value is relevant to the charge storage dynamics: When ${\sf b}=0.5$ , the electrode material presents battery properties and suggests a diffusion-controlled process; when $0.5<\mathsf{b}<1$ , the electrode shows battery properties and pseudocapacitance properties, including both the surface and diffusion-controlled process; when ${\sf b}=1$ , the material exhibits pseudocapacitance properties and indicates a surface capacitive controlled reaction. The b values of the cathodic and anodic peaks are calculated to be 0.8457 and 0.7957 in Fig. 6c, respectively, indicating coexistence of battery and pseudocapacity in $\Nu\mathrm{i}{\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P@P-C N T}$ electrode. Besides, the capacitance contribution is further calculated according to the following formula [44]:
$$
I/\nu^{0.5}\ =\ \mathrm{k}_{1}\nu^{0.5}\ +\ \mathrm{k}_{2}
$$
where $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are the constants, I present the current, $\upsilon$ is the scan rate. The CV curves in Fig. 6d shows the surface capacitive effects in the total specific capacitance are $64.4\%,67.9\%,71.3\%$ and $74.3\%$ at 2, 3, 4 and $5.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , respectively. The pseudocapacitance becomes the dominant contribution in the total capacitance with the increasing scan rate (Fig. 6e), confirming the strong surface redox reaction at high scan rate. This phenomenon may be ascribed to the shortening of ion diffusion distance and the acceleration of electron transportation at high scan rates. Rate performance is actually the response of capacity, retention and recovery of materials under a variety of different rate charge and discharge currents, it is related to the reversibility of the reaction on the material surface, and the reversibility of the reaction is related to the structural stability of the material. The electrodes we designed had porous structure with interconnected network can shorten ion diffusion distance, which is also can promote electron transportation at high scan rates.
A hybrid supercapacitor was assembled to verify the application potential. Fig. 7a is the assembly diagram of the supercapacitor. As we all know, the negative electrode material is one of the vital factors that directly affect the performance of supercapacitors. Selecting a novel binder-free negative electrode material with a preferable capacitance is a promising strategy to optimize the performance of hybrid supercapacitor device. We chose P-doped graphene film intercalated with carbon nanotubes (PGC) as the negative material, the cross-section SEM image of the PGC is shown in Fig. S4a. We can see that carbon nanotubes are evenly interpenetrated in the graphene nanosheets with a thickness of about $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The element mapping (Fig. S4b–f) shows that C and P elements are evenly distributed on the film surface. We further tested the XRD pattern and XPS spectra of PGC in Fig. S8. The broad peak (220) appears at $24.8^{\circ}$ confirms the conversion of GO to rGO after the phosphating process. Besides, Fig. S8c displays a C highresolution spectrum, it can be fitted into three peaks at 284.6, 285.7, and $288.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ which are referred to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}},{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}0$ , and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ vibrations, respectively. It can be seen that the peak intensity $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ of is much higher than $c{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ indicating most of the oxygen-containing groups are removed after phosphating treatment. The three-electrode performance test was performed in the voltage window of $-0.9–0\vee$ (Fig. S5). There is no redox peak in CV curve of PGC (Fig. S4a) demonstrating it is a typical electric double layer material. The maximum capacity can reach $157.8\mathrm{~F~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . CV curves of PGC and $\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P}\ @\mathsf{P-}$ CNT electrodes performed in a three-electrode system was presented in Fig. 7b. It is speculated that the assembled supercapacitor window can be extended to more than $1.4\mathrm{V}$ . The appropriate voltage window of the supercapacitor is further determined by CV curves in Fig. 7c, the curve polarization occurs in the voltage window over $1.7\mathrm{V}$ . But in GCD curves, electrode performance becomes unstable, therefore, test voltage window of $_{0-1.6\mathrm{V}}$ was finally chosen. A series of CV curves of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o P@P-C N T//P G C}$ device in Fig. 7d presents a rectangular shape in the low voltage range and an obvious redox peak in the high voltage range, indicating both electric double layer capacitance behavior and Faraday pseudocapacitance behavior of the electrodes measured under the two-electrode system. Fig. 7e is the GCD curves of the supercapacitor at different current densities under a voltage window of $_{0-1.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ The good symmetry of the curves proves that the device has good electrochemical performance. The gravimetric/volumetric capacity of the device is calculated to be $192.9\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(117.7\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}.\$ ), $177.2\mathrm{~F~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $108.1\ \mathrm{F\thinspacecm}^{-3}.$ , $146.6\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(89.4~\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ), $121.4\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ (74.1 F $\mathsf{c m}^{-3}$ ), $107.5\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(65.6~\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}).$ , and $92.1\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(56.2\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , and ${10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}}$ , respectively (Fig. 7f).
Energy density is another most important indexes to evaluate the performance of supercapacitors. Our device presents a highest gravimetric/volumetric energy density of 68.6 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ (41.8 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) at 800 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ $^{\prime}488\mathrm{W}\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ ) compared to those of the previous reported carbon nanotubes based supercapacitors, such as $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - P@CNT//AC (33.5 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $387.5~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}\d t.$ [50], ${\mathsf{C o P/C N F/C N T/}}/{\mathsf{I}}$
CoNiP/CNF/CNT (56.0 W $\mathrm{h~kg^{-1}}$ at 1400 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [51], CNT@NiCoLDH//CNT@NC (37.4 W $1~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at 750 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [52], $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}@\mathsf{C N T}//$ AC (25.61 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $810\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ ) [53], CCS//CNC (38.4 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $750\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ ) [54], $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{PANI}/\mathrm{CNT}//\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{PANI}/\mathrm{CNT}$ (13 W $\mathrm{h}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ at 1000 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [55], $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{CNTs}//\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{C}$ NTs (17.4 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at 101.9 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [42], (CNTs $)/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}//(C N T s)/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ (1.17 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ at $2430\textrm{W}\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ [14], $\mathrm{PPy}@\mathrm{CNT}70/\mathrm{BC}30//\mathrm{PPy}@\mathrm{CN}$ T70/BC30 (3.6 W $\mathrm{~h~L~}^{-1}$ at 391.7 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [56], $\mathrm{MnO_{2}/C N T//P P y/C N T}$ (7.88 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at $2260\mathsf{W}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ ) [57]. We further test the cycle stability of $\mathsf{P G C}//\mathsf{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}/\mathsf{C o P@P-C N T}$ under a high current density of $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ in Fig. 8c. After charging-discharging 8000 cycles, the device displays about $84.2\%$ capacitance retention. The surface of the electrode material is also observed by SEM, as shown in Fig. S6. We can see that the surface of the film become rougher but still connected by carbon nanotubes. Therefore, this may be the reason for the decrease of cycle performance of the assembled supercapacitor. Furthermore, to explore its viability and potentiality in practical applications, two supercapacitors in series are employed to light 9 blue and 10 white LEDs with ‘‘M”, which display for $60~s$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a novel self-supporting electrode of P-doped carbon nanotubes intercalating $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}0_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}$ core–shell polyhedron with rich oxygen vacancies were successfully synthesized. $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}$ - ${\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C o P}}$ composites derived from metal-organic frameworks have highly exposed active sites and rich oxygen vacancies for pseudocapacitance. P-doped carbon nanotubes (CNT) have high electrical conductivity can enable the fast transportation of generated electrons to an external circuit from the electroactive material. Thus, the as-designed self-supporting composite film displays an ultrahigh gravimetric/volumetric specific capacitance due to the synergistic effect of the two components. A hybrid supercapacitor assembled with $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C o P}@\mathsf{P}$ -CNT and P-doped graphene/CNT shows an excellent energy density of and long cycle stability. This work may provide a new strategy for fabricating high volumetric/- gravimetric specific capacitance electrodes in the next miniaturization and portable energy storage systems.
